# Every variable the maintenance scripts read, or reach through the modules they
# call, with the default each one falls back to when it is unset. Copy this to
# `.env` and edit, or export them in the shell you run the scripts from — nothing
# here loads a file by itself, and nothing in the package reads an API key: the
# Zen free tier's credential is the literal string `public`.
#
#   bun run check:gate   /   bun run limits:audit   /   bun run capture

# check:gate — which model the gate probes Zen with, and which endpoint it asks.
# ZEN_BASE_URL=https://opencode.ai/inference/v1
# The model the gate probes with. It must be one the route still serves:
# `space-bunny-free` was retired on 2026-10-19, and a probe model that is gone
# makes every run of the gate say nothing about the fingerprint.
# ZEN_CHECK_MODEL=minimax-m3.1-flash-free

# limits:audit — the same endpoint override, plus where the catalogue is read
# from and which captured generation the audit asks as.
# ZEN_BASE_URL=https://opencode.ai/inference/v1
# ZEN_CATALOG_URL=https://models.opencode.ai/api.json
# ZEN_GENERATION=1.x
#
# limits.mjs's own knob, reachable because the audit calls the rule that reads
# it: the two decisions limits.mjs makes without a trace — a value taken from a
# refusal and applied past the staleness guard, an entry the catalogue has moved
# past being void, and now a window the route moved — are reported on stderr when
# this is set, and stay silent otherwise. `limitDiagnostics` is the same sink for
# a host that keeps its own log.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_DEBUG=1
#
# Where the measured walls are read from — the `route-sweep` tier the runtime
# asks alongside its table and the catalogue. Unset means `limits-observed.json`
# beside `limits.mjs`; an empty value means no snapshot at all, leaving the table
# and the catalogue to answer alone. It is read once per process, so a host picks
# up a new one when it next starts.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_SNAPSHOT=./limits-observed.json
#
# The same snapshot as published on the repository's `main`, so a measurement
# the hourly workflow commits reaches a host without an npm release. Unset reads
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tkhs101/OpenCode-Zen-FreeX/main/limits-observed.json
# in the background, at most once per process per refresh interval, and keeps
# the last good answer in `limits-observed.remote.json` under this platform's
# cache directory (atomic, 0600). That cache is read at startup, before the
# first model list, so a restart does not spend a request. An empty URL turns
# the remote off; an empty cache path turns off only the file and keeps the
# answer in memory. Naming the snapshot path above (even as "") also turns the
# remote off, because a source was chosen. The refresh interval is an hour, and
# one fetch may take five seconds.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_SNAPSHOT_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tkhs101/OpenCode-Zen-FreeX/main/limits-observed.json
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_SNAPSHOT_CACHE=
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_SNAPSHOT_REFRESH_MS=3600000
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_SNAPSHOT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
#
# The adaptive limits — the bracket a model's own traffic draws around the wall
# this route enforces — and the three knobs on it. The state is one JSON file,
# written atomically and at most once per debounce window; an empty
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_STATE keeps it in memory for the life of the process,
# which is what the test suite sets and what a host that wants no file at all
# should set too. Unset means the cache directory this platform uses:
# `%LOCALAPPDATA%`, else `$XDG_CACHE_HOME`, else `~/.cache`, then
# `/opencode-zen-freex/limits.json`.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_STATE=
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_STATE_DEBOUNCE_MS=5000
#
# How far the search may declare above what the route has proved, and how often
# it may probe at all. The window cap bounds what the search raises — never a
# value the table or the catalogue declared — and the cooldown spaces the probes,
# one per model per window whichever way the last one went, because a probe that
# overshoots is a refused turn somebody pays for.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MAX_WINDOW=1048576
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_LIMITS_COOLDOWN_MS=900000

# discovery — where a successful free-model listing is remembered. The file
# says which schema wrote it (`version`), when it was read (`listedAt`), and the
# model records and protocol map the route answered. Unset means
# `discovery.json` in this platform's cache directory (`%LOCALAPPDATA%`, else
# `$XDG_CACHE_HOME`, else `~/.cache`), under `/opencode-zen-freex/`; an empty
# value keeps discovery in memory only — nothing is read and nothing is written.
# It is the floor a listing failure falls back to (and what a restart starts
# from), and one live read always wins over it; the write is best effort and
# atomic.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_DISCOVERY_CACHE=

# sessions — one identity is held for a window by default: an OpenCode-shaped
# session id and a project id, minted together, with the current moment as the
# id's timestamp (always inside the window the tier accepts), kept for the
# window below and replaced together when it ends.
#
# The window is per conversation, not per process. The host's name for the
# conversation — the session header the CLI sends on every request, then the
# body's `conversation_id`, `metadata.session_id` or Claude Code user id — picks
# the slot; the name itself is never sent, only the pair minted for it. Two
# conversations running at once therefore hold a pair each, and a 429 rotates
# only the one it refused. A request that names nothing shares one slot: two
# requests nothing can tell apart are two requests the tier cannot either, and
# minting per request there would be the hop burst the window exists to avoid.
#
# The window itself, in milliseconds: 60000 by default, bounded to 86400000 (a
# day), and `0` mints a fresh pair per request — the behaviour this package
# shipped with, and the value to set when measuring one ledger, because a
# rotating id cannot answer a question about one id.
#
# `OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_SESSION_AFFINITY=1` is the alternative: one session per
# conversation — the host's OpenCode-shaped id is passed through, or one is
# derived from the host's session identifiers and remembered, so every turn of
# the same conversation shares an id, and the window is not consulted then.
# Both are read per request; the host honours a change from its next request
# without a reload.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_IDENTITY_TTL_MS=
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_SESSION_AFFINITY=

# the 429 retry — the tier ledgers its free quota per project id and egress
# together, so a spent bucket is not a spent exit: measured 2026-10-26, the same
# exit answered 429 to one project id and 200 to a fresh random one, twice. A
# host ends the whole turn on a 429 and calls the quota exhausted, so a request
# the route would have served under a new ledger used to be a dead turn. Each
# retry mints a project id and a session id nothing has spent and sends the same
# bytes again. Only a 429 is retried — never a 403, a 426 or a 5xx — and a 429
# whose body names abuse of the egress is not retried either, because no id
# answers a blocked IP.
#
# The ledger only exists while the egress has room for it. The shared public
# credential identifies no one, so limits are keyed to what does identify
# traffic — the egress IP, and beneath it the project id — and the egress-level
# budget is spent by everyone on the same exit: measured 2026-10-27 on a
# popular VPN exit whose admission probe had just answered 200, a ~32k-token
# request was refused five times and then a ~5k one five more (and two such
# rounds after those) — twenty fresh project ids, one answer: 429. On a
# saturated exit the retry cannot open a ledger; only time
# can. Measuring your exits is the only way to know which case you are in, and
# it takes real-size requests: a ping-sized probe sees the per-id ledger and
# misses the ceiling entirely.
#
# The first is how many retries: 2 by default, `0` turns the retry off, and the
# ceiling is 5. The second is the wait before the first retry in milliseconds:
# 1200 by default, clamped to 100-120000, doubling per attempt so a route that is
# counting rather than rate limiting is not hammered. All three are read per
# request, so a change takes effect on the next turn rather than at the next
# restart. A value that is not a whole number in range falls back to the default,
# and a wait is abandoned the moment the caller aborts.
#
# The defaults are deliberately short, and they are what a host with nothing
# behind a 429 wants: the bucket is spent, and waiting only postpones the same
# answer. A deployment that knows a longer wait is worth spending sets both
# the first wait and the ceiling for itself — they are read per request for
# that reason. The ceiling never drops below the first wait, so the two cannot
# be set to contradict each other.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_RETRY_429=
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_RETRY_WAIT_MS=
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_RETRY_WAIT_MAX_MS=
#
# A 429 that carries its own window (the `retry-after` header) is ignored by the
# schedule above by default — the short waits are a decision, documented above —
# but `1` hands one attempt's wait to the route whenever it names one, bounded by
# the same 120-second ceiling every wait in this package is. Read per request.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_RETRY_AFTER=
#
# The smallest gap between two requests this loader puts on the wire, in
# milliseconds: 0 by default (off), bounded to 30000. A burst of identity hops
# from one egress is a shape the gate blanket-refuses where the same requests
# spaced about half a second apart pass (measured by zen-proxy against the same
# tier); the retries carry their own waits and need none of this, so the gap is
# for a host that fires its own requests back-to-back and has seen the refusal
# that follows. Read per request.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_PACING_MS=

# the protocol snapshot — which endpoint a model is asked at, as the docs table
# last said it. `OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODELS_SNAPSHOT` is where the bundled copy is
# read from: unset means `models-observed.json` beside `catalog.mjs`; an empty
# value means no bundled copy at all, leaving the bootstrap table as the floor.
# The remote copy is the same file as published on the repository's `main`,
# refreshed by the hourly `catalog.yml` workflow (which reads one docs page — no
# Zen credential is spent on it): unset reads
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODELS_SNAPSHOT_URL's default in the background, at most once
# per refresh interval, and keeps the last good answer in
# `models-observed.remote.json` under this platform's cache directory (atomic,
# 0600), which is read at startup so a restart does not spend a request. The
# precedence for any one id: the live docs, then the discovery cache, then the
# remote answer, then the bundled copy, then the bootstrap table in code.
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODELS_SNAPSHOT=
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODELS_SNAPSHOT_URL=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tkhs101/OpenCode-Zen-FreeX/main/models-observed.json
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODELS_SNAPSHOT_CACHE=
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODELS_SNAPSHOT_REFRESH_MS=3600000
# OPENCODE_ZEN_FREE_MODELS_SNAPSHOT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# capture — the loopback port the capture server binds while a real OpenCode is
# pointed at it, the time the `opencode` child is given to make its request, and
# where the capture is written. It needs Bun and `opencode` on PATH. Nothing
# leaves the machine: the request OpenCode makes is what is being read, not
# answered. `--fingerprint=<path>` overrides the last of the three; unset means
# this repository's own `fingerprint.json`, and an exported-but-empty
# `CAPTURE_FINGERPRINT` counts as unset rather than as the working directory.
#
# The two numbers are read as numbers and checked: one that is not a whole
# positive number — a typo, a fraction, a zero, a negative — or one past the
# tool's own ceiling is refused and the default is used instead, and the name of
# the refused variable is said out loud. A knob that looks set but was never
# read is worse than one that was never set, so "this one was ignored" is a
# sentence the tool prints rather than a silence.
# CAPTURE_PORT=8791
# CAPTURE_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# CAPTURE_FINGERPRINT=./fingerprint.json
